    "original_title": "Solar irrigation doubles production and cuts costs for Afgoye farmers",
    "slug": "solar-irrigation-boosts-yields-cuts-costs-for-afgoye-farmers",
    "summary": "Farmers in Jarran village, Afgoye district, have doubled their agricultural production after switching from fuel-powered to solar irrigation systems, according to local reports.",
    "content": "Farmers in Jarran village, located in the Afgoye district of Somalia's Lower Shabelle region, have reported a significant increase in agricultural output after adopting solar-powered irrigation systems. The shift away from diesel-powered pumps comes as fuel prices continue to strain small-scale farming operations.\n\nMohamed Nur Isaq, who cultivates a four-hectare farm in the village, told Radio Ergo that his production has more than doubled since he installed a solar system in January. Previously, he spent between $100 and $150 for each irrigation cycle, a cost that became increasingly difficult to sustain as fuel prices rose.\n\nThe move to solar energy addresses a long-standing challenge for farmers in the region, where reliable irrigation is essential for crop growth but expensive due to reliance on imported fuel. The adoption of solar technology is seen as a way to reduce operational costs and improve resilience against fuel price volatility.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom maintain close ties to farming communities in the Lower Shabelle region, this development signals a positive step toward economic stability and food security. Increased agricultural production can lead to lower food prices and greater availability of locally grown produce, benefiting both local consumers and diaspora families who send remittances to support relatives.\n\nAdditionally, the success of solar irrigation in Afgoye could serve as a model for other agricultural areas in Somalia, encouraging further investment in renewable energy solutions. For diaspora members looking to contribute to development projects back home, such initiatives offer a tangible and sustainable way to support livelihoods.\n\nThe transition to solar power not only cuts costs but also reduces dependence on imported fuels, contributing to environmental sustainability. As farmers like Isaq experience higher yields, the potential for increased income and improved living standards grows, offering a hopeful narrative for a region often associated with conflict and instability.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Ergo here: https://radioergo.org/en/2026/09/solar-irrigation-doubles-production-and-cuts-costs-for-afgoye-farmers/",
